findings.message:
When encoding a frame whose width is not a multiple of 16 (DXV_ALIGN), dxv_encode() allocates a padded buffer (safe_data) with av_image_alloc() and copies the real frame into it with av_image_copy2(). It then attempts to zero the padding columns added at the right edge of each row with:

memset(safe_data[0] + y * safe_linesize[0] + frame->linesize[0], 0, safe_linesize[0] - frame->linesize[0]);

The offset and length are computed from frame->linesize[0] (the *stride*, in bytes, of the original unaligned buffer, which may itself already include row padding/alignment inserted by the allocator) rather than from the actual pixel data width (avctx->width * bytes_per_pixel). Because frame->linesize[0] is generally >= the real pixel-row byte width, using it as the boundary between 'real data' and 'padding' under- or over-shoots the true data/padding boundary. This leaves a region of the newly allocated safe_data buffer between the true end of pixel data (width * bpp) and frame->linesize[0] uninitialized (whatever av_image_alloc allocated, which is not necessarily zeroed), while the memset instead clears bytes that may already have been valid copied pixel data or leaves other bytes untouched. The uninitialized bytes are subsequently read and encoded by ff_texturedsp_exec_compress_threads(), causing a use of uninitialized memory (detected by OSS-Fuzz/MSan) and a potential information leak of heap contents into the encoded DXV bitstream.

location.snippet:
if (avctx->width != DXV_ALIGN(avctx->width)) {
    for (int y = 0; y < avctx->height; y++) {
        memset(safe_data[0] + y * safe_linesize[0] + frame->linesize[0], 0, safe_linesize[0] - frame->linesize[0]);
    }
}

findings.suggestion_code:
if (avctx->width != DXV_ALIGN(avctx->width)) {
    av_assert0(frame->format == AV_PIX_FMT_RGBA);
    for (int y = 0; y < avctx->height; y++) {
        memset(safe_data[0] + y * safe_linesize[0] + 4*avctx->width, 0, safe_linesize[0] - 4*avctx->width);
    }
}
